  experiment:{
    title:"External features of the earthworm",
    aim:"To examine and record the external structures of a segmented worm.",
    materials:["Live earthworm","Hand lens","Petri dish","Moist paper towel","Ruler","Drawing paper"],
    steps:[
      "Place the earthworm in a Petri dish lined with moist paper and allow it to settle.",
      "Observe the direction of movement to establish the anterior (front) end.",
      "Count the segments and locate the swollen clitellum.",
      "Run a finger gently along the underside from back to front to feel the chaetae.",
      "Record the length and make a labelled drawing showing mouth, clitellum, segments and anus.",
      "Return the worm to damp soil after the observation."
    ],
    expect:"The body is cylindrical and divided into many ring-like segments. The anterior end is more pointed and carries the mouth. The clitellum is a smooth swollen band about one third along the body, which secretes the cocoon in reproduction. Small bristles called chaetae can be felt on the underside and grip the soil during movement.",
    why:"The earthworm shows the annelid body plan: a segmented cylindrical body with chaetae for grip, a clitellum for reproduction, and a distinct anterior end — the level of organisation is far higher than in hydra, with true organs and a complete gut."
  },
  worked:[
    {q:"A woman's last menstrual period began on 3 March and her cycle is exactly 28 days. On what date is ovulation expected, and when is the next period due?", steps:[
      "Ovulation occurs on about day 14 of the cycle",
      "Day 14 counting 3 March as day 1: 3 + 13 = 16 March, so day 14 is 16 March",
      "The next period begins on day 1 of the next cycle: 3 March + 28 days = 31 March"
    ], a:"Ovulation is expected on about 16 March; the next menstrual period is due on 31 March"},
    {q:"A man ejaculates 3 cm³ of semen in which there are 80 million sperm per cubic centimetre. Find the total number of sperm released.", steps:[
      "Total = volume × concentration",
      "3 × 80 million"
    ], a:"240 million sperm in one ejaculation — of which only one fertilises the egg"},
    {q:"A human body cell has 46 chromosomes. State the number of chromosomes in a sperm nucleus, an egg nucleus, a zygote and the primary endosperm nucleus of a flowering plant.", steps:[
      "Gametes are haploid, so half the body cell's number: 46 ÷ 2",
      "The zygote restores the diploid number by fusion",
      "The endosperm nucleus is triploid: three haploid sets fused — two polar nuclei and one male nucleus"
    ], a:"Sperm 23; egg 23; zygote 46; the primary endosperm nucleus 3n — in maize, where 2n = 20, it would be 30"},
    {q:"A pollen grain measures 25 µm across. What is its apparent size at a magnification of 400×?", steps:[
      "Apparent size = real size × magnification",
      "25 µm × 400 = 10 000 µm",
      "10 000 µm = 10 mm"
    ], a:"10 mm across in the field of view — clearly visible"},
    {q:"A tomato fruit contains 200 seeds. What is the least number of pollen grains that must have fertilised its ovules, and how many male nuclei were used?", steps:[
      "Each seed comes from one fertilised ovule, so at least 200 pollen grains germinated",
      "Each pollen grain supplies two male nuclei in double fertilisation"
    ], a:"At least 200 pollen grains, using 400 male nuclei — 200 fusing with the egg nuclei and 200 with the pairs of polar nuclei"},
    {q:"A woman is 26 weeks pregnant. Convert this to months and state what stage of development the baby is in.", steps:[
      "Full term is 40 weeks; 26 weeks is about 6 months",
      "The embryonic stage ends at 8 weeks, so at 26 weeks the baby is a foetus"
    ], a:"About 6 months; the foetal stage — the organs are formed and growing, and the mother can feel movements"},
    {q:"Sperm production needs a temperature of about 35 °C. If the body temperature is 37 °C, by how much must the scrotum cool the testes, and why is this necessary?", steps:[
      "Difference = 37 − 35"
    ], a:"By about 2 °C; the scrotum hangs outside the abdomen so the testes are cooler than the body core, and at body temperature spermatogenesis stops and the man becomes infertile — the reason tight clothing and prolonged heat are avoided"}
  ],
  apply:[
    {q:"A village has a high rate of Ascaris infection. Suggest three practical control measures.", a:"Build and use latrines so faeces do not contaminate soil and water, provide safe drinking water and hand-washing points, and treat those infected while teaching washing of vegetables before eating."},
    {q:"Why is the tapeworm's lack of a gut not a disadvantage to it?", a:"It lives immersed in the host's already-digested food in the intestine and absorbs nutrients directly through its body surface, so a gut would be redundant."},
    {q:"Explain why eating undercooked pork can cause tapeworm infection.", a:"The pig is the intermediate host and its muscle may contain the larval cysts; if not killed by thorough cooking, the larvae develop into adult worms in the human intestine."},
    {q:"A learner says worm infection is only a matter of dirtiness. Give a fuller explanation.", a:"It is chiefly a matter of sanitation and infrastructure — the absence of latrines, safe water and footwear — which are community and economic problems, not merely personal habits."},
    {q:"How does the level of organisation of hydra differ from that of an earthworm?", a:"Hydra has only two cell layers and a single body cavity with one opening, and lacks true organs; the earthworm has true tissues, organs and organ systems with a complete gut having mouth and anus."}
  ],
