  experiment:{
    title:"Demonstrating that exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide",
    aim:"To compare the carbon dioxide content of inhaled and exhaled air, and the effect of exercise.",
    materials:["Two boiling tubes","Limewater","Delivery tubes and a two-hole stopper assembly","Mouthpiece","Stop clock"],
    steps:[
      "Set up two tubes each containing equal volumes of limewater, connected so that breathing in draws air through tube A and breathing out passes air through tube B.",
      "Breathe gently in and out through the mouthpiece for two minutes.",
      "Record the time taken for the limewater in each tube to turn milky.",
      "Repeat the experiment with a fresh set-up after running on the spot for two minutes.",
      "Compare the times taken in all cases and tabulate the results.",
      "Do not suck limewater into the mouth — take care with the direction of the tubes."
    ],
    expect:"The limewater through which exhaled air passes turns milky within a short time, while that through which inhaled air is drawn stays clear far longer. This shows exhaled air contains much more carbon dioxide, produced by respiration in the tissues. After exercise the limewater turns milky faster still, because the higher rate of respiration in the muscles produces carbon dioxide more rapidly.",
    why:"Respiration in the tissues produces carbon dioxide, which is carried in the blood to the lungs and breathed out. Limewater turns milky because carbon dioxide forms insoluble calcium carbonate. Exercise raises the respiration rate, so carbon dioxide is produced faster."
  },
  worked:[
    {q:"The kidneys filter 180 litres of glomerular filtrate in a day and produce 1.5 litres of urine. What percentage of the filtrate is reabsorbed? If the person drinks a great deal and passes 2.5 litres of urine, what percentage is reabsorbed then?", steps:[
      "Reabsorbed = 180 − 1.5 = 178.5 litres",
      "Percentage = (178.5 ÷ 180) × 100",
      "With the large intake: reabsorbed = 180 − 2.5 = 177.5 litres",
      "Percentage = (177.5 ÷ 180) × 100"
    ], a:"99.2 % reabsorbed normally, and 98.6 % when the water is in excess — the kidney alters the amount of water it keeps by the action of the ADH, not the amount it filters, which is nearly constant"},
    {q:"A person breathes 16 times a minute with a tidal volume of 500 cm³. Find the volume of air moved per minute and per hour, and the volume that actually reaches the alveoli if 150 cm³ of each breath stays in the air passages (the dead space).", steps:[
      "Minute volume = 16 × 500 = 8 000 cm³ = 8 litres per minute",
      "Per hour = 8 × 60 = 480 litres",
      "Alveolar ventilation = 16 × (500 − 150) = 16 × 350"
    ], a:"8 litres a minute and 480 litres an hour moved in and out, but only 5.6 litres a minute reaches the alveoli — which is why the slow deep breathing exchanges more air than the fast shallow breathing of the same minute volume"},
    {q:"One mole of glucose releases 2870 kJ in aerobic respiration, and about 38 molecules of ATP are formed, each storing 30.6 kJ. Find the energy captured in the ATP, the efficiency of the process and the energy lost as heat.", steps:[
      "Energy captured = 38 × 30.6 = 1162.8 kJ",
      "Efficiency = (1162.8 ÷ 2870) × 100",
      "Energy lost as heat = 2870 − 1162.8"
    ], a:"About 1163 kJ captured, an efficiency of about 40.5 %, and about 1707 kJ (59.5 %) lost as heat — a far higher efficiency than any engine built by people, and the heat is not wasted, since it maintains the body temperature"},
    {q:"Compare the energy obtained from one molecule of glucose by the aerobic and by the anaerobic respiration, using 2870 kJ and 150 kJ and 38 and 2 molecules of ATP.", steps:[
      "Ratio of the energy = 2870 ÷ 150",
      "Ratio of the ATP = 38 ÷ 2"
    ], a:"The aerobic respiration yields about 19 times as much energy and 19 times as much ATP from the same glucose — which is why the muscle can only work anaerobically for a minute or two and must then repay the oxygen debt"},
    {q:"The inhaled air contains 21 % oxygen and the exhaled air 16 %. What proportion of the oxygen taken in is absorbed by the blood?", steps:[
      "Oxygen removed = 21 − 16 = 5 %",
      "Proportion absorbed = (5 ÷ 21) × 100"
    ], a:"About 23.8 % of the oxygen in the inhaled air is absorbed — the rest is exhaled, which is why the rescue breathing given to a patient still supplies enough oxygen to keep them alive"},
    {q:"The kidneys receive about 22 % of the cardiac output. If the cardiac output is 5 litres a minute, find the renal blood flow per minute and the total volume of blood that passes through the kidneys in a day.", steps:[
      "Renal blood flow = 22 % of 5 = 1.1 litres per minute",
      "Per day = 1.1 × 60 × 24"
    ], a:"1.1 litres a minute, and about 1584 litres a day — the whole of the body's 5 litres of blood is filtered through the kidneys about 300 times a day, which is why the kidney is so vulnerable to the fall in the blood pressure and to the poisons carried in the blood"},
    {q:"A patient with the diabetes mellitus has a blood glucose of 2.4 g per litre, while the kidney threshold is 1.8 g per litre. Explain what will be found in the urine and why the patient is thirsty and passes much urine.", steps:[
      "The blood glucose is above the threshold, so the tubules cannot reabsorb all of it",
      "The glucose remains in the tubular fluid and appears in the urine",
      "The glucose in the tubule holds the water there by osmosis, so less water is reabsorbed"
    ], a:"Glucose will be present in the urine (glycosuria) together with a large volume of dilute urine; the loss of the water dehydrates the blood, so the osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus cause the thirst and the ADH release, and the patient drinks and passes much urine in a cycle — the dipstick test on the urine shows the glucose, and the blood test confirms the diagnosis"}
  ],
  apply:[
    {q:"A patient's urine contains protein. What does this suggest about the kidney?", a:"The glomerular membrane is damaged and is allowing large plasma proteins through, which normally cannot pass during ultrafiltration."},
    {q:"Explain why a sprinter continues to breathe deeply for minutes after a 100 m race.", a:"Anaerobic respiration during the sprint built up lactic acid; the extra oxygen taken in afterwards oxidises it, repaying the oxygen debt."},
    {q:"Why does a smoker develop a persistent cough and frequent chest infections?", a:"Smoke paralyses and destroys the cilia lining the airways, so mucus and trapped particles are not swept out; they accumulate, provoking coughing and providing a site for bacteria to multiply."},
    {q:"On a hot day a person produces little, dark urine. Explain in terms of homeostasis.", a:"Water is lost in sweat, so the blood becomes more concentrated; ADH is released and the collecting ducts reabsorb more water, producing a small volume of concentrated urine to conserve water."},
    {q:"Why can yeast be used to make both bread and alcohol?", a:"In anaerobic respiration yeast converts sugar to ethanol and carbon dioxide; the carbon dioxide raises the dough in baking, while the ethanol is the product wanted in brewing."}
  ],
