  experiment:{
    title:"Investigating the effect of detergent pollution on plant growth",
    aim:"To find whether detergent in water affects the germination and growth of seedlings.",
    materials:["Bean or maize seeds","Four labelled pots of the same soil","Detergent solution at three concentrations plus plain water","Measuring cylinder","Ruler","Recording table"],
    steps:[
      "Plant five seeds at equal depth in each of four pots and label them: water only, 1% detergent, 5% detergent, 10% detergent.",
      "Water each pot daily with 50 cm³ of its assigned solution, keeping all other conditions identical.",
      "Record the number of seeds that germinate in each pot each day for two weeks.",
      "Measure and record the height of each seedling every three days.",
      "Note the colour and condition of the leaves in each pot.",
      "Plot mean height against time for the four treatments and compare."
    ],
    expect:"The pot watered with plain water shows the highest germination rate and the tallest, healthiest seedlings. As detergent concentration rises, germination is delayed and reduced, growth is stunted and leaves become yellow and may die. This shows that detergent is a pollutant which damages plants, and that the harm increases with concentration — which is why household and industrial effluent should be treated before release.",
    why:"Detergent is a pollutant that damages cell membranes and disrupts water uptake by the roots. The harm increases with concentration, which is the reason household and industrial effluent must be treated before it is released into soil or waterways."
  },
  worked:[
    {q:"A river has a dissolved oxygen of 8 mg per litre above the town and 3 mg per litre below the outfall of the sewage. Find the percentage fall and explain what it indicates.", steps:[
      "Fall = 8 − 3 = 5 mg per litre",
      "Percentage = (5 ÷ 8) × 100",
      "The oxygen has been used by the bacteria decomposing the organic matter of the sewage"
    ], a:"A fall of 62.5 %, indicating a high biochemical oxygen demand and therefore a serious organic pollution: the fish needing the high oxygen — the mayfly nymph, the stonefly, the trout-like species — disappear, and only the tolerant bloodworm and the sludge worm remain, which is how the biologist judges the pollution without any instrument"},
    {q:"A village of 400 people uses 20 litres of water each a day. Find the daily volume needed, and the mass of chlorine required to give a dose of 2 mg per litre.", steps:[
      "Daily volume = 400 × 20 = 8 000 litres",
      "Chlorine = 8 000 × 2 mg = 16 000 mg = 16 g"
    ], a:"8000 litres a day and 16 g of chlorine — and the dose must leave a small residual in the water, since it is the residual that kills the organisms picked up in the container and the pipe between the treatment and the drinking, which is why the water is tested for the residual chlorine and not only at the plant"},
    {q:"A community of 1000 people has 850 immune to the measles after the vaccination campaign. Find the coverage and state whether the herd immunity is reached, given that the measles requires about 95 %.", steps:[
      "Coverage = (850 ÷ 1000) × 100",
      "Susceptible = 1000 − 850 = 150",
      "Immune needed for 95 % = 950",
      "Additional to be vaccinated = 950 − 850"
    ], a:"85 % coverage, which is below the 95 % needed, so 150 people remain susceptible and the measles can still spread among them and reach the infant too young to be vaccinated; 100 more must be vaccinated to reach the herd immunity, which is why the supplementary campaign goes from house to house to find the missed children"},
    {q:"Liberia has about 4.2 million hectares of forest and loses it at about 0.4 % a year. Find the area lost per year and how long it would take to lose half of the forest at that rate.", steps:[
      "Area lost per year = 0.4 % of 4 200 000 = 16 800 hectares",
      "Half the forest = 2 100 000 hectares",
      "Time = 2 100 000 ÷ 16 800"
    ], a:"16 800 hectares a year, and 125 years to lose half of it at that constant rate — a generation and a half. Since the loss in fact accelerates as the road opens the forest and the population grows, the real figure is shorter, which is the argument for the replanting, the improved stove and the payment for the standing forest"},
    {q:"A town of 250 000 people produces 0.5 kg of refuse each per day. Find the mass produced per day and per year, and the volume it occupies if the loose refuse has a density of 200 kg per cubic metre.", steps:[
      "Per day = 250 000 × 0.5 = 125 000 kg = 125 tonnes",
      "Per year = 125 × 365 tonnes",
      "Volume per day = 125 000 ÷ 200"
    ], a:"125 tonnes a day, about 45 625 tonnes a year, occupying 625 cubic metres a day — a heap the size of a large house every day, which is why the collection, the composting of the organic part, the recycling and the engineered landfill are essential, and why the burning of it in the open poisons the air of the whole town"},
    {q:"In a district there are 5000 cases of the diarrhoea in the children under five each year. Washing the hands with the soap reduces the diarrhoeal disease by about 40 %. How many cases would be prevented?", steps:[
      "Cases prevented = 40 % of 5000"
    ], a:"About 2000 cases a year prevented by the washing of the hands with the soap — an intervention costing a few cents, needing no clinic, no drug and no cold chain, which is why the handwashing facility and the teaching of the habit at the school and the health centre are among the most valuable things a community can do"},
    {q:"A fishery yields 2000 tonnes a year but the stock is falling and the young fish are being caught. The catch is reduced by 30 % for three years, after which the stock recovers enough for the catch to be raised by 50 % above the original. Find the catch in each period and explain the principle.", steps:[
      "Reduced catch = 2000 × 70 % = 1400 tonnes",
      "Recovered catch = 2000 × 150 % = 3000 tonnes",
      "The gain after the recovery = 3000 − 2000"
    ], a:"1400 tonnes for three years, then 3000 tonnes a year — a permanent gain of 1000 tonnes a year for three years of the sacrifice. This is the maximum sustainable yield: the catch must be kept below the rate at which the stock replaces itself, and the mesh must be large enough to let the young escape to breed, since the fisherman who catches every young fish this year has no fish next year"}
  ],
  apply:[
    {q:"A mining company discharges silt into a river used by a village. State three consequences.", a:"The silt blocks fish gills and kills aquatic life; it makes the water unsafe and unpleasant for drinking and washing; and it settles on the bed destroying breeding grounds and reducing the village's fish catch."},
    {q:"Suggest three ways a Liberian community with no waste collection can manage its refuse.", a:"Separate and compost biodegradable food and plant waste for use on farms; collect and reuse or recycle plastics, metal and glass; and dispose of the remainder in a properly sited covered pit away from water sources rather than burning it in the open."},
    {q:"Why is protecting a rainforest more valuable than the timber it contains?", a:"The living forest holds soil against erosion, regulates rainfall and rivers, absorbs carbon dioxide, and shelters biodiversity and medicinal species — services that continue indefinitely, while the timber can be sold only once."},
    {q:"A parent refuses to have her child immunised, saying the disease is rare now. Explain the flaw.", a:"The disease is rare precisely because most children are immunised; if enough parents refuse, herd immunity collapses and the disease returns, striking the unvaccinated first."},
    {q:"Explain how planting trees along a river bank benefits both the water and the soil.", a:"The roots bind the soil and prevent erosion, so less silt enters the river; the trees also filter runoff, provide shade that keeps the water cool and oxygenated, and create habitat for wildlife."}
  ],
